68 #edb/ref 17592186413775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413787 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39270" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413787 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413787 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413787 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "05d28970-6cfe-5fc6-bc98-6f13f1fbcff5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413787 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{2} \\\\ 7{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{2}=0 \\\\ 7{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n7{c}_{1}-0=14\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution for the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413788 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413788 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413788 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413788 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413788 #edb/ref 1069 "q-326296" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413788 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413788 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{2t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413788 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "216d1aeb-34d0-53e9-bbe2-39e35d3f0d63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413788 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ {c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-5{c}_{1}=-5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3\\cdot 1+{c}_{2}=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413789 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413789 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413789 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413789 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413789 #edb/ref 1069 "q-326297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413789 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413789 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{2t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413789 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "514febb9-898e-5a8c-8e7f-718f51a9867b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413789 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=2 \\\\ {c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n3{c}_{1}=9\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3+{c}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413790 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413790 #edb/ref 1069 "q-327159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413790 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413790 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned},\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413790 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5cca3049-b571-5225-b8ec-fa2863b439e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413790 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=1$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]e & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5}{c}_{1}+3e{c}_{2} \\\\ {e}^{5}{c}_{1}+e{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5}{c}_{1}+3e{c}_{2}=5{e}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{5}{c}_{1}+e{c}_{2}=3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4e{c}_{2}=8{e}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{2}=2e$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5}{c}_{1}+e(2e)=3{e}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}={e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{-3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+2e[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413791 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413791 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79729" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413791 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413791 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $x(t),$ and that $x(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nfind the position of the particle at $t=3$ seconds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413791 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7ea76f4-22db-56a5-acbc-fee92d706737" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413791 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=3$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=2 \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n-{c}_{2}=6\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+(-6)=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=10.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =10\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+(-6)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $x(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3) & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3\\cdot 3}+[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]{e}^{9}+[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-6} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10{e}^{9}+12{e}^{-6} \\\\ 10{e}^{9}-6{e}^{-6}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
